      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-306",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Patient-data API published on :8000 with no authentication or Host validation; the localhost bind is the only access control.\n\nThe backend router handlers have no auth dependency, and main.py adds only CORSMiddleware, with no TrustedHostMiddleware or Host check. The compose file publishes the API on 127.0.0.1:8000, and the docs (data-safety SKILL.md:12, README) treat that bind as the sole protection. A web page the doctor opens can reach the port through DNS rebinding or, for body-less POSTs, plain cross-site requests. Browsers treat a rebound page as same-origin, so the CORS allow-list does not apply.\n\nImpact: Disclosure and modification of patient records (names, phones, symptoms, prescriptions, fees) by any web page the clinic user visits, plus access by any other local user or process on the machine.\n\nExploit scenario: The doctor, with `make up` running, visits an attacker-controlled site such as evil.example. The attacker's DNS first returns the attacker IP, then rebinds the name to 127.0.0.1. The page's JavaScript calls evil.example:8000/patients and /patients/{id}, which uvicorn serves for any Host header. The attacker reads patient names, phones and prescriptions, and can POST, PATCH, PUT or DELETE records. The page can also fire a blind cross-site POST /visits/{id}/restore, because that request has no body and needs no preflight. The frontend dev server on :5173 is not exposed this way: the lockfile pins vite 5.4.21, which has the allowedHosts check and restricted CORS defaults. Port 8000 is exposed.\n\nPreconditions: The stack is running via docker compose; The victim browses to an attacker-controlled page while it runs; The attacker's DNS rebinding is not filtered by the victim's resolver or browser; Alternatively, the attacker is a local user or process on the host.\n\nRecommendation: Add real authentication, even a shared secret or token that the Vite proxy injects. At minimum, add Starlette TrustedHostMiddleware (allowed_hosts of localhost and 127.0.0.1, plus the compose service name backend) to the backend. Do not publish port 8000 to the host at all, because the frontend reaches the backend over the compose network through the /api proxy.\n\nCWE-306. Severity MEDIUM, confidence low."
          },
