        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-346",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "No Host/origin validation lets a malicious web page reach the unauthenticated localhost API (DNS rebinding / cross-site simple requests).\n\nThe API has no authentication and no TrustedHostMiddleware, so its only barrier against the doctor's own browser is the browser's same-origin policy. A page on an attacker-controlled hostname that is rebound to 127.0.0.1 would be same-origin with the backend and could read and write all PHI. CORS is not a server-side check, and no CSRF or Host check exists.\n\nImpact: Remote attackers can drive the local clinic API through the victim's browser, giving PHI disclosure and record tampering, if the browser's private-network and local-network access protections do not block it.\n\nExploit scenario: The doctor visits a malicious page. (1) The page can fire a body-less cross-site POST at http://localhost:8000/visits/N/restore, which undeletes soft-deleted visits and needs no preflight. (2) With a DNS-rebinding hostname that resolves to 127.0.0.1, the uvicorn app accepts any Host header, so the page becomes same-origin and can GET /patients, PATCH patients, PUT or DELETE visits and read the responses.\n\nPreconditions: Victim browses to an attacker-controlled page while the clinic app is running; For the rebinding variant, the browser does not block public-to-loopback requests; For the CSRF variant, the attacker can guess sequential visit IDs.\n\nRecommendation: Add TrustedHostMiddleware(allowed_hosts=[\"localhost\",\"127.0.0.1\",\"backend\"]) and require an auth token (custom header or cookie with SameSite) on all routes. JSON-body routes already require Content-Type: application/json (FastAPI strict_content_type default in the locked 0.143.1), but body-less POST routes such as restore are not covered by that.\n\nCWE-346. Severity LOW, confidence low."
          },
